# Autoregressive BPTT Trajectory Optimization

Transitioning from 1-step point prediction to Sequence Trajectory Forecasting explicitly proves whether our model has discovered the true generative physics (Wilson-Cowan basins) versus just "lazy" next-step tangent mappings. We will rewrite the computational graph to run sequence rollouts.

## Proposed Changes

### `config/default.yaml`
- Add a new hyperparameter into the `training` block: `sequence_length: 50`. This sets our integration window to 500ms for continuous trajectory simulation.

### `src/datasets.py`
- We will rewrite `NeuropixelsDataset` to extract contiguous sequence chunks instead of single instances.
  - **Inputs:** `x0` (initial state, shape `[2]`), `u_seq` (stimulus series over window, shape `[seq_len, 1]`)
  - **Target:** `x_seq` (ground-truth series over window, shape `[seq_len, 2]`)
- The sliding window step will ensure sequences don't improperly leak.

### `src/models.py`
- All models (`LinearBaseline`, `MLPBaseline`, `MechanisticModel`, `HybridModel`) will have their `forward()` architecture fundamentally refactored into recursive loops.
  - They will be given exactly $x(0)$ and no future ground truth!
  - Inside `forward()`, a PyTorch autoregressive `for` loop will act as the numerical integrator. It calculates step 1, passes its *own output* back into itself for step 2, and so forth for all 50 steps.
  - They will return the full stacked 3D sequence: `[batch_size, seq_len, 2]`.

### `src/train.py`
- Because we unroll the steps inside the PyTorch `forward()` call, calling `.backward()` natively invokes **Backpropagation Through Time (BPTT)**! The gradients will flow perfectly uninterrupted backwards across all 50 generated time steps, physically steering the $W_{EE}$ constraints to guarantee limit-cycle stability.

### `src/evaluate.py` & `src/visualize.py`
- Visualizations will plot these simulated 500ms forecasted chunks versus the true data.

## User Review Required
> [!IMPORTANT]
> A `sequence_length` of **50 bins** (500 ms) is typically strong enough to capture full E/I cycle phases without exploding gradients entirely on early train epochs. Are you okay with unrolling the BPTT for 50 steps, or would you prefer a shorter (e.g. 20) or longer (e.g. 100) trajectory window?
